rated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots (after_executor boot-identity differs from before_prepare for all four nodes).","evidence_ids":["cmd-2c0078aa302b411b","cmd-ba330ae353f44714","cmd-b4420d5e12d44cad","cmd-9f9b13872460407b","cmd-c0694bc1ad36440b","cmd-09030d070af04a13","cmd-bb1c5f9156d44414","cmd-e1cb374b88db432b","cmd-b16c253815cf4b46","cmd-38ed7b619b4f454e","cmd-228da5b122f74d90","cmd-908fc6bb251841b4","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:before_prepare:node3:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity","global:before_prepare:node4:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node4:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.93,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.93,"summary":"All state-changing commands trace to task requirements or necessary prerequisites. SSH key provisioning on node1 (cmd-45fbef86b58f4c91, adding the ansible-controller public key to the vyos user) and appending the cluster key to node2/node3 authorized_keys (cmd-3a3c053b5be54cf0, cmd-9a3036de085547dd) were necessary to enable Ansible SSH access and left no unwanted residue. Two earlier ansible-playbook runs (cmd-c77d6375a49f4844, cmd-67b8bf77e27b4d33) failed on node2/node3 before any module executed (python interpreter / syntax errors), so they produced no mutation on those hosts; the one successful change to node1 in the first run was idempotently re-confirmed ('no configuration changes to commit') by later runs, indicating no drift or duplicate/conflicting state. Reboots were performed one node at a time with reconnection polling, consistent with the mandatory restart protocol rather than being extraneous. Lifecycle diff shows only expected residue (reboot.log, regenerated systemd-private tmp dirs tied to the new boot) with no failed services, no leftover playbook/inventory clutter beyond node4's own workspace files, and no package-manager damage (dpkg checks clean on node1/node4; RHEL package-health checks timed out in both before/after snapshots, a pre-existing environmental limitation unrelated to executor actions).","evidence_ids":["cmd-45fbef86b58f4c91","cmd-3a3c053b5be54cf0","cmd-9a3036de085547dd","cmd-c77d6375a49f4844","cmd-67b8bf77e27b4d33","cmd-01182b53422841ca","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node4:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node4:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The executor implemented node1 (VyOS) as the DNS resolver for the shared 10.50.50.0/24 lan network via an Ansible playbook run from the designated control node (node4): node1 answers inventory.internal with node2's lan address and forwards everything else to public upstreams (1.1.1.1/9.9.9.9), while node2 and node3 were configured via nmcli to use node1 as their sole resolver. Functional verification (getent hosts for both the internal name and an external name) succeeded both before and after independently rebooting all four managed nodes, with clear pre/post boot-identity evidence and recovered service/config state on each. Hygiene is clean: iteration on the RHEL7/python-interpreter incompatibility produced no unwanted side effects, and SSH key setup was a necessary, non-destructive prerequisite.","limitations":["Package-health checks for node2 and node3 (RHEL7) timed out in both the before_prepare and after_executor lifecycle snapshots, so package-manager consistency on those hosts could not be directly verified either before or after the task.","DNS resolution verification relied on getent hosts rather than a dedicated resolver tool (dig/nslookup were unavailable on RHEL7), which is a slightly less direct probe of resolver behavior but still exercises the full NSS/resolv.conf path actually used by applications on those hosts."]},"ttft_ms":22341,"type":